A graph is not a tree, so seeing the same value again does not mean a new node should be made. The dictionary uses the original node object as the key, which is safer than using only values. Each cloned neighbor is attached while the original node is processed.

The self-loop case is covered too. Since the clone is placed in the dictionary before its neighbors are checked, a neighbor pointing back to the same node receives that same clone.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public Node CloneGraph(Node node) {
        if (node == null) {
            return null;
        }

        var clones = new System.Collections.Generic.Dictionary<Node, Node>();
        var stack = new System.Collections.Generic.Stack<Node>();

        clones[node] = new Node(node.val);
        stack.Push(node);

        while (stack.Count > 0) {
            Node current = stack.Pop();

            foreach (Node neighbor in current.neighbors) {
                if (!clones.ContainsKey(neighbor)) {
                    clones[neighbor] = new Node(neighbor.val);
                    stack.Push(neighbor);
                }

                clones[current].neighbors.Add(clones[neighbor]);
            }
        }

        return clones[node];
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(V + E)` because each node is discovered once and each neighbor entry is copied once.

Space complexity: `O(V)` for the dictionary and stack. The original graph remains unchanged.

This version separates discovery from connection building. First, a stack finds every node that can be reached and creates an empty clone for it. After that, a second pass copies all neighbor links. Keeping these two jobs separate makes the edge copying easy to follow.

An empty input is handled before the stack is created. A graph with a self-loop also works because the node is already in the map when its neighbors are connected.

```javascript
class Solution {
    cloneGraph(node) {
        if (node === null) {
            return null;
        }

        const clones = new Map();
        const stack = [node];
        const order = [];

        clones.set(node, new Node(node.val));

        while (stack.length > 0) {
            const current = stack.pop();
            order.push(current);

            for (const neighbor of current.neighbors) {
                if (!clones.has(neighbor)) {
                    clones.set(neighbor, new Node(neighbor.val));
                    stack.push(neighbor);
                }
            }
        }

        for (const current of order) {
            const copiedNode = clones.get(current);

            for (const neighbor of current.neighbors) {
                copiedNode.neighbors.push(clones.get(neighbor));
            }
        }

        return clones.get(node);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`, with one pass for discovery and one pass for the neighbor lists.

Space complexity: `O(V)` for the map, stack, and list of discovered original nodes.

This uses a stack instead of recursion. The important part is putting a new node into the map as soon as it is discovered. That means a back edge can immediately point to the right cloned object.

Dry run on two connected nodes: node 1 is mapped to `1'` and pushed. Its neighbor 2 gets `2'` and is pushed. While processing 2, the edge back to 1 uses the already stored `1'`.

```java
class Solution {
    public Node cloneGraph(Node node) {
        if (node == null) {
            return null;
        }

        java.util.Map<Node, Node> clones = new java.util.HashMap<>();
        java.util.ArrayDeque<Node> stack = new java.util.ArrayDeque<>();

        clones.put(node, new Node(node.val));
        stack.push(node);

        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            Node current = stack.pop();

            for (Node neighbor : current.neighbors) {
                if (!clones.containsKey(neighbor)) {
                    clones.put(neighbor, new Node(neighbor.val));
                    stack.push(neighbor);
                }

                clones.get(current).neighbors.add(clones.get(neighbor));
            }
        }

        return clones.get(node);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`.

Space complexity: `O(V)` for the map and explicit stack. This avoids possible recursion depth problems, and it leaves the original neighbor lists untouched.

The graph can have cycles, so a clone must be stored before its neighbors are visited. The map connects each original node pointer to its new node. When DFS reaches an old node again, it returns the saved clone instead of creating another one.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    Node* cloneGraph(Node* node) {
        unordered_map<Node*, Node*> clones;
        return copy(node, clones);
    }

private:
    Node* copy(Node* node, unordered_map<Node*, Node*>& clones) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        if (clones.count(node)) {
            return clones[node];
        }

        Node* clone = new Node(node->val);
        clones[node] = clone;

        for (Node* neighbor : node->neighbors) {
            clone->neighbors.push_back(copy(neighbor, clones));
        }

        return clone;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`, since every reachable node and neighbor connection is processed.

Space complexity: `O(V)` for the map and the recursive call stack. The input graph is not changed.

A queue makes it easy to visit the graph one layer at a time. I create the starting copy first, then add each original neighbor to the queue only when its clone does not exist yet. The same dictionary also prevents cycles from causing repeated work.

For example, with `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 1`, node 1 is copied first. Node 2 and node 3 are added next, and when node 1 is seen again, its existing clone is reused.

```python
from collections import deque

class Solution:
    def cloneGraph(self, node):
        if node is None:
            return None

        clones = {node: Node(node.val)}
        queue = deque([node])

        while queue:
            current = queue.popleft()

            for neighbor in current.neighbors:
                if neighbor not in clones:
                    clones[neighbor] = Node(neighbor.val)
                    queue.append(neighbor)

                clones[current].neighbors.append(clones[neighbor])

        return clones[node]
```

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`.

Space complexity: `O(V)` for the dictionary and queue. The empty graph returns `None`, and a single node with no neighbors is copied correctly.

